BEFORE (passed all tests, failed production):

async function reserve(itemId, qty) {
  const item = await db.items.find(itemId);
  if (item.stock < qty) throw new Error('OOS');
  await db.items.update(itemId, { stock: item.stock - qty });
  return { reserved: qty };
}

two requests read stock=5, both pass check, both write stock=3. sold 4, only had 2.

AFTER (same interface, serializable):

async function reserve(itemId, qty) {
  const result = await db.transaction(async (tx) => {
    const item = await tx.items.lockForUpdate(itemId);
    if (item.stock < qty) throw new Error('OOS');
    await tx.items.update(itemId, { stock: item.stock - qty });
    return { reserved: qty };
  });
  return result;
}

select for update locks the row. second request waits, sees stock=3, fails correctly.

THE VULNERABLE PATTERN

async function increment(key) {
  const current = await redis.get(key)
  const next = (current || 0) + 1
  await redis.set(key, next)
  return next
}

Reads are fast. Writes are fast. The gap between them is where the bug lives. Under load that gap widens.

THE FIX: COMPARE AND SET LOOP

async function increment(key) {
  while (true) {
    const current = await redis.get(key)
    const next = (current || 0) + 1
    const ok = await redis.set(key, next, 'NX', 'XX')
    if (ok) return next
  }
}

Redis SET with NX XX acts as compare and set. If the value changed since our GET the SET fails and we retry. No external locks, single key, linearizable.

THE TEST CODE (JAVA, JUNIT 5, CYCLICBARRIER):
```java
@RepeatedTest(100)
void doubleSpendRace() throws Exception {
  var barrier = new CyclicBarrier(2);
  var latch = new CountDownLatch(2);
  executor.submit(() -> transfer(50, barrier, latch));
  executor.submit(() -> transfer(50, barrier, latch));
  latch.await();
  assertEquals(0, repo.findBalance(id));
}
```
THE TRANSFER METHOD READS BALANCE, HITS BARRIER.AWAIT(), THEN CHECKS AND WRITES.
